hi I'm David Newland and thanks for  visiting my poster it's all my project  is about a new way of measuring order  and disorder in tumors and how this can  be applied as a new diagnostic tool to  improve precision medicine to start the  architectural organization of cells  inside a tumor is highly disorganized  such that most people would say that the  arrangement of cells is random however  there's a difference between randomness  and disorder when we when we say that a  system is random what we are saying is  that what we're assuming is that we have  sufficient knowledge of what is  non-random in that system such that we  can say that both you know this tumor  the cells are arranged randomly  however you know from engineering  computer science we know that you know  the entire premise of encrypting email  and digital information that sent over  Internet right is premise upon our  ability to actually make something you  know something that's ordered to look so  disordered right that it's you know  incomprehensible and thus random okay  and so there is existing evidence if you  look at figure one that tumors do have  an inherent order to them that we have  been aware for a long time this is why  tumors can be graded by a pathologist as  grade 1 grade 2 or grade 3 being the  most say aggressive and the grading  system of tumors is based upon the  features such as nuclear morphology you  know the pink or purple mists of the  hematoxylin and eosin stain in the  tissue excuse me and other factors and  so and furthermore pathologists are able  to classify tumors according to their  histo  biological subtype for example a  pathologist can identify a tumor as you  know a adenoid carcinoma while another  tumor might be a you know cystic right  carcinoma another one might be  metaplastic okay so metaplastic  carcinomas or metaplastic breast cancers  are ones in which the structure  architecture within a tumor right is  highly variable as it is not just one  type or one recurring type of structure  it's multiple ones mixed together right  so it's very hard to can be very hard to  classify and metaplastic  tumor right but the fact that there is  tumor grading and histopathological  subtypes right is strong indication that  though tumors are highly disorganized  right there are recurring themes and  regularities that scientists can  identify and use to stratified cancers  in to varying degrees of say  aggressiveness or severity so the  purpose of my research is to refine that  ability such that we can classify these  tumors even further into more specific  groups that allows us to then determine  you know which types of tumors respond  to therapy better what type of therapy  and you know which type tumors  ultimately relapse more than others in  order to do this for metaplastic tumors  I created what's called the quadrant  slope index so in order to if you look  at theater to write the the way you  would do the Quadra slope index or QSI  is to divide the entire image into a  grid of uniform squares okay and then  each of these squares you then further  divided into you know four even squares  right with you know a two-dimensional XY  coordinate and so with each of these  within each of these X Y you know  quadrants or each of these squares has  four quadrants right that you know  result from an XY axis right you can  simply trace the  the longest length of each nuclei right  and the slope of that line will either  point up right be positive slope or  point down be a negative slope right  with the bottom of image being the  reference ok the horizontal parallel  with or a horizontal x-axis so in this  way in each of these quadrants you can  count the number of you know upward  slopes and a number of downward slopes  ok and such that you can divide a one  over the other right such as in Figure  two see in this way not only can you  generate a number okay for each quadrant  that is in each of these squares in the  larger grid right these numbers can also  be turned into a color right in the form  of a heat map so in this way by the  Quadra slope index method right you can  generate a new view of a tumor image if  you look at figure three here's a  hypothetical example in figure 3 a here  is you know typically a cartoon of what  you know a metaplastic tumor would look  like right this is a garbled arrangement  of cells of different types and  orientations in figure 3 B here's a  hypothetical grid that has been created  and each of the red axis right is one of  the squares in the in the grid of the  whole tumor all right and each of the  quadrants resulting from the red axis  right can become a color so in this way  if you look at a figure 3a right it was  purposely drawn to have an obvious  pattern of cells in the upper right hand  corner of 3a that is the spindly  stretchy elongated cells that flow  together you know like a stream of water  say a stream of liquid okay and so  that's sort of easily detected that's  who think by the human eye and also by  the quadrant slope index method right as  seen in figure 3  see right the the green squares right  labeled as over obvious right however a  lot of people are surprised that there's  another hidden I guess order in figure 3  a that is the bottom right hand corner  right you have these oval shaped cells  that are arranged in a row right but to  the human eye is really hard to pick  that out okay especially if you're  looking under a microscope as  pathologists do or now even on the  screen as pathologists in the United  States are allowed to diagnose and you  know according to the FDA now this is  several months ago diagnosed you know  the tumors and tissues by looking at  computer screens right and so so people  are often surprised to realize that wow  yeah so figure three a does have two  regions of orderliness right the obvious  one and the the letter H in three C  figures 3 C that is the hidden right  region order right and so that's the  power of this quantitative method the  Quadra slope index and that because it  is calculated with the help of a  computer that can you know kept late new  things very quickly and you know at a  high rate we're able to turn the  features right the orderliness and  disorderliness of a tumor into numbers  okay and for the more we can aid the  pathologist in by visualizing the degree  of order and disorder right in each  tumor right so this allows for more  refined stratification of tumors okay  instead of just grade 1 grade through to  grade 3 we can create grade 1 a 1 B 1 C  2 a 2 B 2 C etc right and so this is  exciting in that this method has the  potential to enhance the precision  diagnostic oncology so that when you  your physician takes a biopsy of your  tumor and sends it to a pathologist the  pathologist can give a more specific  recommendation as to the stage of your  disease say and which therapy would best  help you with the fewest side effects  it's also exciting in that you know we  the next stage is to test whether if we  can calculate you know the QSI instead  of use a computer what is the minimum  number of Q sis or squares right and  make you more image that we can  calculate and still accurately predict  the outcome right of that that patient  at that sample that would be highly  useful in the case where people can only  have access to low-tech diagnostic tools  whether that's in the case of developing  nations or when you know the hurricane  shuts down electricity right and we you  know our batteries our apps our  computers run out of electricity what do  we do right you know then we rely and  the old school method of just you know  pen and paper so to speak and so when we  when we have a large you know there's a  lot of large archive data sets of tumor  images that also correspond with the  patient data as to no age and Z stage  and how long they lived and how long  they live before relapse happen right  now such clinical information and so  having that data w